00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:33.660
With the benchmark gap clear, let's look at a strategy that sidesteps exploration risk entirely.

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:39.760
Royalty acquisitions led investors capture cash flow exposure to operating mines

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:42.860
without funding exploration or development risk.

00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:49.140
Summit royalties just closed a 1% net smelt return on Newmont's Saddle North deposit

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:51.840
for 5 million Canadian dollars in shares.

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:56.200
Giving direct participation in future production at a fixed cost.

00:01:56.780 --> 00:02:00.400
This structure works best when the underlying asset has proven reserves

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:03.320
and the royalty holder maintains low overhead.

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:09.360
Investors can screen TSX venture royalty companies using free cash flow yield

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:14.900
and reserve life metrics on platforms like TMX Money or stockanalysis.com.

00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:19.080
The approach historically shines in rising commodity cycles,

00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:23.940
but carries single asset concentration risk if the mine underperforms.

00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:30.680
Position sizing below 5% of a TFSA portfolio limits drawdown if metal prices stall.

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:32.980
Royalty's reward discipline.

00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:36.580
Now let's apply similar discipline inside your TFSA.

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:41.600
Imagine you bought a high growth Canadian tech name last week inside your TFSA

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:44.220
expecting 25% annual returns.

00:02:44.900 --> 00:02:49.920
Your order filled cleanly, yet the real edge comes from understanding that every dollar of growth

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:56.220
compounds completely tax free while the same holding in a taxable account would lose 26.5%

00:02:56.220 --> 00:03:00.140
to capital gains tax on exit. The mechanism is straightforward.

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:06.460
Contribution room resets every January 1st and unused room carries forward indefinitely,

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:13.720
but over contributions trigger a 1% per month penalty that can erase months of gains on even a $5,000

00:03:13.720 --> 00:03:19.900
mistake. Professionals always check their CRA my account balance before any large purchase

00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:25.240
and keep a running adjusted cost-based spreadsheet to avoid superficial loss traps on re-entries.

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:32.720
The biggest mistake with TFSAs is parking only low yield GICs inside them while high growth assets

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:39.820
sit in taxable accounts. Instead, always allocate the highest expected return holdings to the TFSA

00:03:39.820 --> 00:03:44.720
first, then use the remaining room for income-producing names that would otherwise trigger

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:51.120
withholding taxes on US dividends. That TFSA edge pairs well with today's momentum catalyst.

